<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Roundtree returns six days after first coming in with a painless lump under the left nipple. Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound of both breasts and both axillae the next day showed a 1.6 cm irregular mass eccentric to the left nipple and one abnormal left axillary lymph node; the right breast and axilla were normal. Core biopsies of the mass and the node the same day show invasive ductal carcinoma, grade 3, estrogen receptor 95 percent, progesterone receptor 85 percent, HER2 negative, with cancer in the node. He was asked to come in so the result could be given in person. He says he had not thought a man could get breast cancer and had been sure it was the breast enlargement he had at 13. He has no bone pain, cough, headache, abdominal pain, or weight loss. He saw Dr. Siddiqui of breast surgical oncology this morning, and the operation is booked in two days. His main worries are the operation, whether he can keep working, and keeping the diagnosis private. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>Invasive ductal carcinoma, left breast, grade 3, ER and PR positive, HER2 negative; left axillary node positive</td></tr><tr><td>Other breast and axilla</td><td>Normal on mammography and ultrasound</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms of spread</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Coordination</td><td>Surgical oncology seen today; operation in two days</td></tr><tr><td>Concerns</td><td>The operation, work, and privacy</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

